Matrix Chain Multiplication (MCM)

⚡ Classic DP
⚡ Interval DP Pattern
⚡ Asked in Amazon / Microsoft / Google

🧩 Problem Statement

You are given an array arr[] of size n.

If you have matrices:

A1 = arr[0] x arr[1]
A2 = arr[1] x arr[2]
A3 = arr[2] x arr[3]
...


Find the minimum number of scalar multiplications needed to multiply all matrices.

🔎 Example

Input:

arr = [10, 30, 5, 60]


Matrices:

A1 = 10x30
A2 = 30x5
A3 = 5x60

Possible ways:

1️⃣ ((A1 × A2) × A3)
2️⃣ (A1 × (A2 × A3))

Calculations:

1️⃣
A1 × A2 → 10×30×5 = 1500
Then × A3 → 10×5×60 = 3000
Total = 4500

2️⃣
A2 × A3 → 30×5×60 = 9000
Then × A1 → 10×30×60 = 18000
Total = 27000

✅ Minimum = 4500

🧠 Core Idea

This is:

🔥 Interval DP
We break problem into partitions.

Formula:

dp[i][j] = min over k:
dp[i][k] + dp[k+1][j] + arr[i-1] * arr[k] * arr[j]

⏱ Time Complexity

O(N³)

Space: O(N²)

💻 Java Code (Tabulation)
class Solution {

    static int matrixMultiplication(int[] arr) {

        int n = arr.length;
        int[][] dp = new int[n][n];

        // l = chain length
        for (int l = 2; l < n; l++) {

            for (int i = 1; i < n - l + 1; i++) {

                int j = i + l - 1;
                dp[i][j] = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

                for (int k = i; k < j; k++) {

                    int cost = dp[i][k] 
                             + dp[k + 1][j]
                             + arr[i - 1] * arr[k] * arr[j];

                    dp[i][j] = Math.min(dp[i][j], cost);
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[1][n - 1];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {10, 30, 5, 60};
        System.out.println(matrixMultiplication(arr));
    }
}

